<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.15.3: The Coordinates of the Vertex</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02012</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.15.3: The Coordinates of the Vertex</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9ed905d5-0632-4298-80de-f0e0b36edee8</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=x^2\). Then, add different numbers to \(x\) before it is squared (for example, \(y=(x+4)^2\), \(y=(x−3)^2\)) and observe how the graph changes.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Adding a number to \(x\) moves the graph to the left. Subtracting a number (or adding a negative number) moves the graph to the right. The vertex stays on the \(x\)-axis.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph \(y=(x−1)^2\). Then, experiment with each of the following changes to the function and see how they affect the graph and the vertex.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Add different constant terms to \((x−1)^2\) (for example: \(y=(x−1)^2+5\), \(y=(x−1)^2−9)\). </item>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem> 
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem></exercise>
</example><div class="os-raise-ib-content" data-schema-version="1.0" data-wait-for-event="Reveal2">
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para>Adding a constant term moves it up and subtracting moves it down. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex doesn't change.</para>
</div>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Multiplying \((x−1)^2\) by different coefficients (for example: \(y=3(x−1)^2\), \(y=-2(x−1)^2\), \(y=0.5(x−1)^2\). </para>
    <para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your Answer:</para>
  <para> Multiplying the squared term by a number doesn't change the vertex, but it changes the opening of the graph to "wider" or "narrower," or changes the direction of the graph to open upward or downward.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="6729b2e3-5c54-4823-b887-7a8cc6a79cda" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q3a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="f0b34626-53c2-444d-bdc8-ff755e0788a6" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-10">
    <problem>
      <para>Without graphing, find the coordinates of the vertex of \(y=(x+10)^2\) and determine if it opens up or down.</para>
      <para> What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is -10.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="78115021-dd69-4349-a6b0-ee2dbb7a4169" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="0">
    <problem>
      <para> What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is 0.</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="fa086711-1ff1-437c-a416-95dafb171f2b" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Up" data-solution-options="[&quot;Up&quot;, &quot;Down&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> Does the graph open up or down? </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient (1).</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Do not edit below line.-->
  <solution>
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </div>
</div>

