</example><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Are the patterns you noticed still happening? See if you can apply those patterns to the following equations.</item>
</list>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="7681aab6-d9ba-4b23-93b4-e86b6f9c219c" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q7a-->
  <exercise data-content-id="8fd2bb2b-35a9-49b3-93e0-6b1010b68b27" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-3">
    <problem>
      <para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–3\).</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q7b-->
  <exercise data-content-id="97021ac7-5208-432a-a6f5-432c6448485f" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-5">
    <problem>
      <para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(-5\).</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q7c-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1d9cfcdf-595c-471d-9307-16dd20ce2f36" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Up" data-solution-options="[&quot;Up&quot;, &quot;Down&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), does the graph open up or down?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient \(\frac{1}{2})\). </para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q7d-->
  <exercise data-content-id="16199d79-2505-408f-b4ff-e5b931fa83bb" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="-100">
    <problem>
      <para> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex? </para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–100\).</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q7e-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a1771807-d7bb-465d-9019-eabb1fce4737" data-problem-comparator="float" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="50">
    <problem>
      <para> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(50\).</para>
    </solution>
        </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q7f-->
  <exercise data-content-id="7fc8f227-f6be-48a3-97bf-c9787f891f76" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Down" data-solution-options="[&quot;Up&quot;, &quot;Down&quot;]">
    <problem>
